<p><tt>/scp-5144</tt> (A Multiversal <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Joy</span>ride) by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wolfmandeth" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6859403); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6859403&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606783027" alt="Wolfmandeth" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6859403)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wolfmandeth" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6859403); return false;">Wolfmandeth</a></span> deleted at -20 and three votes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Item #:</strong> SCP-5144</p>
<p><strong>AKA:</strong> A Multiversal <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Joy</span>ride</p>
<p><strong>Object Class:</strong> Safe | Euclid | Keter</p>
<p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> SCP-5144 is <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">3</span> <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">4</span> 5 separate entities/objects. 5144-2 and 5144-3 have been contained but 5144-1 cannot be contained by any means in this reality (See SCP-5144-1 log for details).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Feedback ported:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;from&nbsp;Quicksilvers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I can see that you've put a lot of effort into this article since its a real long one, but there is a lot that can be rewritten or reworked.</p>
<p>SCP nicknames are never actually referenced in the main document. It usually only there on the mainlist or when in casual conversation - no actual SCP document refers to the anomalies by their nicknames. The syntax is off throughout the article and isn't clinical enough to read as an actual SCP document too. You use a lot of images here but a lot of them don't seem to be licensed under creative commons. That means that you using them in this article infringes upon the original creators' copyright.</p>
<p>The dialogue is a little too stiff for segments like the breaches and interviews. By &quot;stiff,&quot; I mean that it doesn't sound that realistic? The scientists either seem too robotic or too casual depending on the scenario, and they don't really speak like analytical scientists.</p>
<p>Another thing about your piece is that the format of the actual document is very jarring and doesn't fit the normal style of an SCP report. You explain the anomaly too much in the Special Containment Procedures when it should actually be described in a &quot;Description&quot; subsection that is missing entirely. The lack of a main Description segment also doesn't help since some readers immediately jump to the Description segment of an SCP document without reading the Containment Protocols - no main description could be a deterrent to people who open the article and glance to see there isn't something concise.</p>
<p>Now, the sub-anomalies are a little all over the place. Each sub-anomaly has so much to it that they each could possibly be categorized as their own SCP within themselves instead of cramming them all into a single document. I'd advise that if you're planning to revise and keep going with this concept, you split the whole document up into multiple different SCP a la DJKaktus's <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-4812">fey</a> <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-2254">war</a> <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-4840">mythos</a>. I can see you're probably aiming for an interesting interwoven story like <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-4812">4812</a>, but everything sort of collides into each other instead of meshing together. You also have a slight tendency to have these really strange descriptions - with -1, its over-described, yet with -2, -3, and -4, they're under-described. There's also things sprinkled in throughout the document that don't fit the bill - the &quot;cry for help&quot; broadcast in another language, the unnecessary crosstests with popular SCPs, and whatever&#8230; <em>this</em> is&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5144-1 has no sexual attraction to humans. However, it has shown interest in SCP-1472, claiming that Dr. Bright said something about Velociraptors in maid costumes. SCP-5144-1 has sent 4 requests to Dr. Paul Blart for permission to enter SCP-1472, permission has been denied.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't know, it just doesn't sit well with the reader.</p>
<p>That's all I really have to say - needs a LOT of reworking but it can eventually be fleshed out into a good article.</p>
<p>Best of luck!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;from&nbsp;Mikaelious</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This is probably longer than any article on the site. While length isn't necessarily a bad thing, you're gonna have to make it more than captivating if you don't want the readers to lose interest.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
